            {
              "id": "glog-d38b9a1a-7d1a-4d31-9d5b-c638efa881cb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, it can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other unexpected behavior. This can potentially be exploited to execute arbitrary code, cause a denial of service, or leak sensitive information.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(hipComplex))`, the size of the memory to be allocated is determined by the size of the `hipComplex` type. If the `hipComplex` type is not defined or its size is not properly calculated, it can lead to the aforementioned issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and correctly calculate the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using `malloc` with user-supplied sizes or sizes that can be influenced by an attacker. Instead, use fixed sizes or sizes based on trusted data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `hipComplex` is a struct or class, you should ensure its size is correctly calculated. If `hipComplex` is not defined, you should define it before using it with `malloc`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\nstruct hipComplex {\n    // Define the members of the struct here.\n};\n\n// ...\n\nhipComplex* complex = (hipComplex*) malloc(sizeof(hipComplex));\nif (complex == NULL) {\n    // Handle the error.\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are required for the provided code snippet.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links are valid and active at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
